(But note: after we assign operator to origin, operator becomes unavailable; we need to assign to destination as well, requiring another operator resource. We only have one operator_resource (driver_operator_alpha). So we cannot have both origin and destination linked to operator simultaneously because operator would be unavailable after first assignment. However lock actions require candidate_operator_linked; after locking we could release operator? lock does not affect operator link. So we need operator linked for both origin and destination at the time of locking each. Since we only have one operator, we need to lock one, then release operator, then link to other, lock it. Release operator action: release_operator_resource ?candidate ?operator_resource. Effect: operator_available, not candidate_operator_linked. So we can release after lock.)
(Thus sequence: prepare origin, assign operator to origin, lock origin (sets lock+confirmed), release operator from origin, then prepare destination, assign operator to destination, lock destination, release operator.)
(But we also need to load/unload cargo before lock? Load requires not locked. So we need to load before lock. Actually load requires not origin_availability_locked. So we must load before locking. However after load we have lock true (from load effect) but not confirmed. Then we need to get confirmed via unload (which does not require lock). Unload does not require lock? Let's check unload_origin_payload precondition: candidate_prepared, candidate_task_profile_linked, origin_availability_node_linked, origin_node_occupied, origin_payload_linked, not origin_availability_confirmed. No lock condition. So we can unload while locked? The precondition does not mention lock; it's okay if locked is true. So we can load (sets lock true, not confirmed), then unload (sets confirmed true, lock stays true). Good. So we need to load before lock? Actually after load lock is true; we can still unload (since unload does not require not lock). So we can load, then unload, then lock? But lock action requires candidate_prepared, candidate_operator_linked, origin_availability_node_linked, origin_node_ready, not origin_availability_locked. Wait lock_origin_availability precondition includes NOT origin_availability_locked. So we cannot lock after we have locked via load. So we need to lock before loading? Let's examine lock_origin_availability precondition: candidate_prepared, candidate_operator_linked, origin_availability_node_linked, origin_node_ready, not origin_availability_locked. So we need origin not locked to lock. Load sets lock true, so we must lock before loading. But load also sets lock true (so after lock we would have lock true twice? Actually lock action sets lock true; load also sets lock true (no conflict). But load precondition requires not locked, so we cannot load after lock. So order must be: lock first (requires not locked), then load? But load requires not locked, so cannot load after lock. So we cannot have both lock and load if lock makes locked true and load requires not locked. However load effect also sets lock true; but precondition prohibits lock. So we cannot load after lock. So we need to achieve locked and confirmed without load setting lock? But load sets lock as side effect; maybe we can avoid load and achieve occupied node via other means? How to get origin_node_occupied without load? There's no other action that sets origin_node_occupied except load_origin_payload. So we need load to get occupied. Load sets lock true as side effect, but also requires not locked precondition. So we cannot have lock true before load. Thus we cannot have both lock and occupied via load if lock precondition prohibits load. However maybe we can load cargo, making lock true, then later do some action that sets confirmed while keeping lock true (without requiring not locked). Unload does not require lock, but unload also removes payload link and makes cargo available, but does not affect lock. However unload also sets confirmed true. So after load we have lock true, not confirmed. After unload we have lock true (still), confirmed true, node occupied? Let's verify: after load: node occupied true, lock true, cargo not available, payload linked true. After unload: precondition requires origin_node_occupied true, origin_payload_linked true, not origin_availability_confirmed. Effect: origin_node_ready, origin_availability_confirmed, cargo_available, not origin_payload_linked. Node occupied unchanged (still true). Lock unchanged (still true). So after load then unload we have lock true, confirmed true, node occupied true, node ready true (from unload effect). Cargo becomes available. Perfect! And note load precondition required not locked; we satisfied that because before load lock was false. After load lock becomes true. Then unload precondition does not require not lock, so okay. After unload lock remains true. So we never needed lock action; we achieved lock and confirmed via load then unload. Indeed load sets lock true, unload sets confirmed true, lock remains true. So we don't need lock_origin_availability action at all. Good! Similarly for destination.)
(Thus we can achieve locked+confirmed via load then unload.)
(Now we need to ensure we have candidate_operator_linked for lock? Not needed if we achieve lock via load/unload. But does load/unload require candidate_operator_linked? No. So we can skip operator linking entirely. However goal does not require operator linked. So we can ignore operator linking.)
(Now we need to ensure we have cargo_available for loading; we have cargo_available initially. After loading origin, cargo becomes unavailable; after unload origin, cargo becomes available again. Then we can load destination (cargo becomes unavailable), then unload destination (cargo available). At end cargo_available will be true (since we unloaded destination). That's fine.)
(Now we need to ensure we have candidate_operator_linked not needed.)
(Now we need to ensure we have candidate_prepared for origin and destination (we will do via confirm_candidate_preparedness). Need candidate_task_profile_linked for each before preparing. We'll manage with detach/reattach as earlier.)
(Now we also need to assemble transport leg. For that we need origin_availability_locked and destination_availability_locked (both true after load/unload), origin_availability_confirmed and destination_availability_confirmed (both true after unload), origin_node_ready and destination_node_ready (we will have after activation and load/unload? Let's check). We'll activate origin node (makes ready) then load origin (makes occupied, lock) then unload origin (makes ready, confirmed). So after unload origin, node ready true, occupied true. Good.)
(Similarly destination: activate destination node (makes ready), load destination (makes occupied, lock), unload destination (makes ready, confirmed). So after unload destination, node ready true, occupied true.)
(Thus after these steps we have origin_node_ready, origin_node_occupied, destination_node_ready, destination_node_occupied, origin_availability_locked, origin_availability_confirmed, destination_availability_locked, destination_availability_confirmed.)
(Now we need to assemble transport leg. Which assemble action to use? We have origin_availability_locked, destination_availability_locked, origin_node_ready?, destination_node_ready?, origin_availability_confirmed?, destination_availability_confirmed? The assemble_transport_leg precondition: origin_availability_locked, destination_availability_locked, origin_availability_node_linked, destination_availability_node_linked, origin_node_ready, destination_node_ready, origin_availability_confirmed, destination_availability_confirmed, transport_leg_available. All true after we have done activation and load/unload for both. So we can use assemble_transport_leg.)
(Effect: transport_leg_assembled, transport_leg_origin_linked to origin_node, transport_leg_destination_linked to destination_node, not transport_leg_available. It does not set origin_leg_loaded or destination_leg_loaded. Goal requires transport_leg_assembled, transport_leg_origin_linked, transport_leg_destination_linked (yes). It does not require origin_leg_loaded or destination_leg_loaded. So assemble_transport_leg suffices.)
(Now we need to also have transport_leg_origin_linked and destination_linked set (yes). Good.)
(Now we need to have dispatched shift_candidate_alpha (we will do earlier). Also need vehicle_available vehicle_unit_gamma (we will get after dispatch). Also need dispatch_slot_available dispatch_slot_alpha (after dispatch). Also need vehicle_allocated destination_availability_alpha (we will allocate). Also need candidate_vehicle_linked destination_availability_alpha vehicle_unit_beta (after allocate). Good.)
